## What Is a VS1 Emerald Cut Diamond?

A VS1 emerald cut diamond is a step-cut diamond with a clarity grade of VS1 — Very Slightly Included 1 — meaning inclusions are minor and difficult to see even under 10× magnification. To the naked eye, VS1 emerald cuts are eye-clean in the vast majority of cases.

I'm **Farzana Hasan**, a GIA-certified gemologist. I've graded and evaluated emerald cut diamonds across every clarity level. The questions about VS1 vs VS2 and emerald cut sparkle are the two things buyers get most wrong — and they're connected in a way most guides don't explain clearly.

The emerald cut is a step-cut diamond with a large rectangular table and long, parallel facets arranged in tiers. It has typically 57 facets, like a round brilliant — but where a round brilliant uses those facets to create constant scintillation, the emerald cut uses them to create long rectangular reflections. This facet difference is the reason clarity rules are stricter for emerald cuts.

---

## The Clarity Window

The Clarity Window is the reason you cannot use round brilliant clarity rules when buying an emerald cut diamond.

A round brilliant has 57–58 facets cut in triangular and kite shapes across the crown and pavilion. These tiny facets create rapid, overlapping light reflections — the sparkle pattern that moves across the stone as it rotates. Any inclusion inside a round brilliant gets visually interrupted by this constant scintillation.

The sparkle pattern masks it.

An emerald cut has the same number of facets — but they are arranged in long, parallel rectangular steps. These flat planes reflect light as broad, undivided mirrors rather than dozens of tiny sparkle points. When you look into an emerald cut, you are looking through large, clear planes of glass into the interior of the stone.

There is no sparkle pattern to interrupt your view.

The Clarity Window is this: the emerald cut's step-cut facets create a visual pathway straight into the diamond. Any inclusion positioned in or near the table — the large flat top — sits in direct view, with nothing to hide it. An inclusion that would be invisible at SI1 in a round brilliant can be plainly visible at VS2 in an emerald cut.

> **Farzana says:** "The Clarity Window is the most important concept in emerald cut buying and almost no retail salesperson explains it. Buyers come in saying 'my friend has an SI1 round brilliant and it looks perfect,' and they think they can do the same in emerald cut. You can't. The facet structure is completely different. In a round brilliant, the sparkle is literally hiding the flaws. In an emerald cut, you're looking straight through a series of mirrors into the stone. Same inclusion, completely different visibility."

The Clarity Window also explains why emerald cuts cost less per carat than round brilliants. The open facet structure requires better rough diamond quality to begin with — more of the rough stone material is discarded to remove inclusions before cutting. But the cutting itself is less labor-intensive than the precise mathematical faceting of a round brilliant.

The result: emerald cuts trade the brilliance premium for a clarity premium.

---

## The VS1 Floor

The VS1 Floor is the clarity grade below which emerald cut buying becomes a stone-by-stone inspection task rather than a reliable grade-based decision.

At VS1, inclusions in an emerald cut diamond are minor enough — and positioned favorably enough in the vast majority of GIA-graded stones — that eye-clean status is reliably achieved without needing to inspect the specific clarity plot. You can [buy a VS1 emerald cut diamond](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds/emerald-cut?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) with confidence that what you receive will be eye-clean.

At VS2, eye-clean status depends on inclusion position. A VS2 stone with a small included crystal near the girdle edge — away from the open table — can be eye-clean. The same VS2 grade with a crystal positioned under the center of the table is not.

Two VS2 emerald cuts with identical GIA grades can look completely different — one eye-clean, one not.

This is why the VS1 Floor exists as a concept rather than a guarantee. It is the grade at which the random variation in inclusion position stops mattering for naked-eye clarity. Above VS1 (VVS2, VVS1, IF, FL), you're buying grades the naked eye cannot detect at any viewing angle.

Below VS1 (VS2 and lower), you're relying on stone-specific inspection.

For buyers who want to select their stone online — which is how most diamond purchases now happen — VS1 is the safe floor. The only way to buy VS2 safely online is through retailers who provide 360° video and a clarity plot for each specific stone, allowing you to verify inclusion position before purchase.

