## The Sparkle Trade

The most common question about emerald cut diamonds is some version of: "do they sparkle?" The answer is yes — but not in the way round brilliants do.

The Sparkle Trade is the exchange you make when you choose a step-cut diamond. You give up the constant scintillation of a brilliant-cut facet pattern. You get architectural flash and the hall-of-mirrors effect instead.

In a round brilliant, the 57–58 tiny facets create rapid, overlapping light returns as the stone moves. Hundreds of small flashes of white light and colored fire dart across the stone continuously. This is scintillation — the signature sparkle of brilliant-cut diamonds.

In an emerald cut, the long rectangular step facets create a completely different visual. Instead of hundreds of tiny flashes, you get large rectangular planes of reflected light that turn on and off as the stone rotates. The interior of the stone reflects itself in long horizontal bands — the "hall of mirrors" effect.

When the stone catches light, it produces a single large rectangular flash rather than continuous small ones.

> **Farzana says:** "The Sparkle Trade question always comes from buyers who've been told emerald cuts 'don't sparkle.' That's wrong. They sparkle differently. A round brilliant is a chandelier — constant light from every angle. An emerald cut is an art deco mirror — still, clear, and then suddenly brilliant when the light hits it right. Buyers who choose emerald cut love that single dramatic flash. It's not a compromise. It's the point."

The Sparkle Trade also explains the Clarity Window relationship. The hall-of-mirrors visual depends on long, clear reflective planes. Those same clear planes are what make inclusions visible.

You cannot have the hall-of-mirrors effect without also accepting the Clarity Window effect. They come from the same facet structure.

This is why emerald cuts are often described as a more "sophisticated" or less flashy look — not because they lack sparkle, but because their sparkle is less continuous and more architectural. The person who chooses an emerald cut is typically choosing it specifically for this reason.

---

## Can You See Through an Emerald Cut Diamond?

Yes — this is the hall-of-mirrors effect in action, not a flaw.

When you look straight down into a well-cut emerald cut diamond from above, you see the interior reflecting itself. The rectangular facets on the pavilion (bottom) reflect back up through the table (top), creating the appearance of an infinite corridor of mirrors. This is completely normal and is the defining visual of the cut.

"Light leakage" is different from "seeing through" the diamond. Light leakage refers to areas of the diamond where light exits through the pavilion instead of returning to the eye — these appear as dark, dead zones when viewing from above. In a well-proportioned emerald cut, light leakage is minimal.

In a poorly proportioned one — particularly those cut too shallow or with a depth percentage below 60% — light leakage creates dark central areas that undermine the hall-of-mirrors effect.

The [ideal proportions for an emerald cut diamond](/emerald-cut-diamond-ideal-proportions) to minimize light leakage are a depth of 60–70% and a table of 60–72%. Stones outside these ranges are more prone to light leakage regardless of clarity grade.

Light leakage and clarity are separate issues. You can have a VS1 emerald cut with poor light return if the proportions are off. You can have a VS1 emerald cut with excellent light return and the hall-of-mirrors effect fully present.

Always check both the GIA clarity grade and the stone's depth/table percentages when evaluating an emerald cut.

---

## How Important Is Color in an Emerald Cut Diamond?

Color matters more in emerald cuts than in round brilliants, for the same reason clarity does: the Clarity Window effect applies to color as well.

In a round brilliant, the constant scintillation breaks up and masks slight body color. An H or even I color round brilliant can appear white to the naked eye because the sparkle pattern interrupts your perception of the base color. In an emerald cut, the long flat facets reflect the body color of the stone directly.

A slight yellow tint in an H or I color stone is more visible in an emerald cut than in a round brilliant of the same grade.

The general rule is to go one color grade higher in an emerald cut compared to what you would choose in a round brilliant:

