## Is a Flawless Emerald Cut Diamond Worth It?

Almost never.

A Flawless (FL) or Internally Flawless (IF) emerald cut diamond costs a significant premium over VS1. [FL and IF emerald cuts](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds/emerald-cut?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) carry 30–60% higher prices than VS1 stones of the same carat weight and color.

The naked eye cannot distinguish FL from VS1 in an emerald cut. Under the Clarity Window effect, VS1 inclusions are minor and positioned in ways that do not affect face-up appearance. The upgrade from VS1 to VVS2 to VVS1 to IF to FL is entirely a magnification-level distinction.

The only cases where FL or IF makes sense:

**Heirloom intention.** If the ring is explicitly being bought as a multi-generational piece and the story of absolute clarity is part of the intention, FL has a narrative value. The practical visual difference remains zero.

**Investment holding.** FL diamonds retain a separate collector market. If the diamond is being purchased partly as a store of value, FL carries a different resale logic. This is distinct from buying for wear.

**Rarity preference.** Some buyers simply want the rarest grade regardless of practical visibility. That's a valid choice. It's not a visual choice.

For the vast majority of emerald cut buyers — even those with no budget constraint — VS1 delivers the same face-up appearance as FL at a fraction of the cost. The difference exists only in the grading report.

---

## Are Emerald Cut Diamonds More Expensive?

This question has two opposite answers depending on what you're comparing.

**Emerald cuts are cheaper per carat than round brilliants.** The cutting process for a round brilliant is more precise and wastes more of the rough diamond octahedron material. Emerald cut yield from rough is higher, and the cutting labor is less intensive. Certified market data consistently shows emerald cuts priced 15–25% below round brilliants of the same carat weight, color, and clarity.

A [1-carat VS1 G round brilliant starts around $5,500–$8,000](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds/round?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). A [1-carat VS1 G emerald cut starts around $3,500–$5,500](https://www.bluenile.com/diamonds/emerald-cut?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). That's a meaningful per-carat discount.

**But:** The total cost of an eye-clean, well-colored emerald cut ring can be comparable to a round brilliant ring because emerald cuts need higher clarity and color grades to achieve the same visual result. A buyer who would choose VS2 H in a round brilliant needs to budget for VS1 G or F in an emerald cut. That partially closes the per-carat gap.

**Why do some emerald cuts seem MORE expensive?** Several reasons: premium well-proportioned stones in high-demand sizes (1.5ct, 2ct) command premiums. The increasing popularity of emerald cuts has pushed prices up on the best specimens. And some buyers compare a VS1 F emerald cut to a VS2 H round brilliant — different clarity and color — then conclude the emerald cut is more expensive, when it isn't on a like-for-like basis.

**Are emerald cuts the most expensive diamond cut?** No. Round brilliants command the highest per-carat prices due to the precision of their cutting and the breadth of demand. Fancy shapes — including emerald cut — all trade at a discount to round. Among fancy shapes, elongated cuts in high demand (marquise, oval) can sometimes trade at premiums for specific size ranges.

---

## What Makes a Good Emerald Cut Diamond?

A good emerald cut diamond meets these standards. Not all of them cost more — they're selection criteria within a budget.

**Clarity:** VS1 minimum. VS2 only with stone-specific inspection confirming eye-clean status.

**Color:** G or better for white metal settings. H acceptable in yellow or rose gold. The Clarity Window effect applies to color; emerald cuts show body color more than round brilliants.

**Proportions:** Depth 60–70%. Table 60–72%. Length-to-width ratio 1.40–1.55 for a classic look. These are not arbitrary — they determine light return and the hall-of-mirrors effect. Outside these ranges, light leakage increases and the characteristic emerald cut visual weakens. The full proportions guide is at [Emerald Cut Diamond Ideal Proportions](/emerald-cut-diamond-ideal-proportions).

**Certification:** GIA only for natural diamonds. IGI or GCAL for lab grown. GIA is the strictest grading lab — an SI1 from another lab can correspond to an SI2 from GIA.

**Cut grade:** The GIA does not issue a cut grade for emerald cuts the way it does for round brilliants. Evaluate cut through proportions (depth, table), symmetry, and the 360° video showing light return.

**Setting:** The [right setting for an emerald cut](/emerald-cut-diamond-ring-settings) matters for both clarity hiding and face-up appearance. A four-prong classic solitaire exposes all four corners; a bezel setting or cathedral setting partially covers the corners and can make VS2 inclusions near the corners less visible.

> **Farzana says:** "The question 'what makes a good emerald cut' always has the same answer when I'm grading: does it have the hall-of-mirrors, and is it eye-clean? Those two things. The proportions determine the first. The clarity grade determines the second. If you have both, you have a good emerald cut diamond. If you're missing either, no amount of color grade or certification makes up for it."

---

## Expert Summary

The VS1 emerald cut diamond is the right choice for most buyers because it sits at the exact intersection of eye-clean reliability and price efficiency. VS1 is the VS1 Floor — the grade where you don't need to inspect each individual stone, view a clarity plot, or worry about inclusion position.

The Clarity Window tells you why emerald cuts need higher clarity than round brilliants: the step-cut facets create a direct visual pathway into the diamond. The Sparkle Trade tells you why emerald cuts look the way they do: large rectangular flashes rather than constant scintillation. Neither the Clarity Window nor the Sparkle Trade is a disadvantage — they are the defining characteristics of the cut.

For buyers with flexibility: VS1 G in white metal or VS1 H in yellow gold is the target combination. For buyers on a tighter budget: VS2 with 360° video inspection can save 10–20% while remaining eye-clean. For [lab grown emerald cut diamond](/lab-grown-emerald-cut-diamond) buyers: VS1 in D–F color costs comparatively little more than VS2 and removes all inspection risk.

