## FAQs

### What is a VS1 emerald cut diamond?

A VS1 emerald cut diamond is a step-cut diamond graded Very Slightly Included 1 by GIA — the fourth-highest clarity grade on the scale. Inclusions are minor and difficult to see even under 10× magnification. In the vast majority of VS1 emerald cuts, the stone is eye-clean from normal viewing distances.

VS1 is the recommended minimum clarity for emerald cuts because of the Clarity Window: the open step-cut facets create a clear visual pathway into the diamond where inclusions at lower grades would be visible.

### Is VS1 or VS2 better for an emerald cut diamond?

VS1 is the safer choice for emerald cuts. VS2 can be eye-clean but requires inspecting the specific stone's inclusion position — inclusions near the table center are visible in emerald cuts at VS2 in a way they would not be in a round brilliant.

The price difference is typically 10–20%. At 1 carat, VS1 is worth the premium for the certainty it provides. If budget requires VS2, use a retailer with 360° video and request the clarity plot to verify inclusion position before purchasing.

### Can you see inclusions in a VS2 emerald cut diamond?

Sometimes — it depends entirely on the location of the inclusion within the specific stone. A VS2 inclusion positioned at the girdle edge (the narrow band around the perimeter of the diamond) is far less visible than the same grade inclusion positioned under the center of the table. This is why VS2 requires stone-by-stone inspection in emerald cuts, while VS1 does not.

### Can you see through an emerald cut diamond?

Yes, and this is normal. The hall-of-mirrors effect in an emerald cut creates the appearance of looking through parallel planes of glass into the diamond's interior. This is the defining visual of the cut, not a flaw.

What you should not see is dark, dead zones within the stone — those indicate light leakage from poor proportions. A well-proportioned emerald cut with a depth of 60–70% will have minimal light leakage and strong hall-of-mirrors reflection.

### Why do emerald cut diamonds not sparkle like round brilliants?

Because of the Sparkle Trade. Emerald cuts deliberately use long rectangular step facets rather than the tiny triangular facets of brilliant cuts. The step-cut facets create large rectangular flashes of light rather than continuous small scintillation points.

An emerald cut does sparkle — but it sparks with large, architectural flashes rather than constant rapid movement. This is the visual the cut is designed to produce. It is not a deficiency; it is the design.

### Do emerald cut diamonds sparkle less?

They sparkle differently, not less. A round brilliant produces continuous scintillation — rapid small flashes across all 57 facets as the stone moves. An emerald cut produces intermittent large flashes — single rectangular planes of light turning on as the stone catches a light source.

Buyers who prefer constant movement tend to prefer round brilliants. Buyers who prefer the single dramatic flash and the still, mirror-like face-up appearance tend to prefer emerald cuts.

### Are emerald cut diamonds more expensive than round brilliants?

No — emerald cuts are typically 15–25% cheaper per carat than round brilliants at the same carat weight, color, and clarity grade. The per-carat discount exists because round brilliant cutting wastes more rough diamond material and requires more precise cutting labor.

The total ring cost can be similar because emerald cuts require higher clarity and color grades for the same visual result. But the stone itself — comparing like for like — is cheaper in emerald cut.

### Why are emerald cut diamonds cheaper?

Because the cutting process preserves more of the rough diamond. A round brilliant must be cut from a specific orientation of the rough octahedron crystal with significant material loss. An emerald cut retains more of the original rough weight.

Lower material waste plus lower cutting labor equals a lower per-carat price for the finished stone.

### Why are emerald cut diamonds so expensive?

The better question is why they cost what they do: emerald cuts require high-quality rough material because the Clarity Window leaves little room for inclusions. The cutter must select rough diamonds with few internal characteristics and position the cut to minimize inclusion visibility. That raw material quality requirement pushes base prices up even before the cutting labor is applied.

### Is a flawless emerald cut diamond worth buying?

Not for practical wear. The naked eye cannot distinguish FL from VS1 in any diamond, including emerald cuts. The Clarity Window effect makes VS2 visible — but VS1 and everything above VS1 are all eye-clean.

The FL premium (often 30–60% above VS1) buys a certification-level distinction that has no visual expression in the finished ring.

The only rational cases for FL are: investment/resale logic, heirloom narrative intent, or personal preference for the rarest grade regardless of cost.

### Are emerald cut diamonds the most expensive diamond cut?

No. Round brilliants are the most expensive per carat due to higher demand and more intensive cutting. Fancy shapes — including emerald cut — trade below round brilliant on a per-carat basis.

Among fancy shapes, certain high-demand cuts (oval, marquise in popular size ranges) can approach or briefly exceed emerald cut prices, but emerald cuts are generally in the mid-range of fancy shape pricing.

### What makes a good emerald cut diamond?

Four things: VS1 or better clarity, G or better color in white metal, proportions within 60–70% depth and 60–72% table, and GIA certification. The hall-of-mirrors effect must be present — check for it in 360° video before purchasing.

Proportions matter as much as the clarity grade for the visual result. A VS1 emerald cut with poor proportions will show light leakage and a weak hall-of-mirrors effect. A VS1 emerald cut with ideal proportions will have the full architectural flash the cut is known for.

### Is an emerald cut a good cut for a diamond?

Yes — for the right buyer. The emerald cut is the correct choice if you prefer a large face-up appearance for the carat weight, a rectangular silhouette, the hall-of-mirrors visual, and a more restrained aesthetic than brilliant cuts. It is not the correct choice if you want maximum scintillation and constant sparkle movement — that's what a round brilliant or oval delivers.

The cut itself is a defined step-cut style with a centuries-long history. It is not a compromise cut. It is a specific design language.

